--- Rosetta Code: Accumulator factory
--- https://rosettacode.org/wiki/Accumulator_factory
--- Deviations, as the task asks them described. Crow has no closures and no
--- hidden mutable state, by design (the spec's "No Lambdas or Closures" and
--- "No Ambient Mutability"), so no function can answer a function that keeps
--- a running total (rule 4). Instead the accumulator is a value: a record
--- holding its total, which foo makes; calling it is accumulate[g, i], which
--- answers the accumulator with i added, and the caller keeps what it answers.
--- Nothing global holds the total (rule 5), and every number handed to an
--- accumulator stays in its total (rule 3). Rule 2 bends: an int or a float
--- is decided before the program runs, so where any accumulator is handed a
--- float every total is a float (a whole float still shows as an int).

struct Accumulator [total]

fn foo[n]
    return Accumulator[n]

fn accumulate[g, i]
    return Accumulator[g.total + i]

x = foo[1]
x = accumulate[x, 5]
_other = foo[3]
x = accumulate[x, 2.3]
log[x.total]
